## Final Judgement
The key insight from the fact-check is that Scientific American published an opinion piece by Joel M. Moskowitz titled “We Have No Reason to Believe 5G Is Safe,” which explicitly warns about potential health risks. However, the magazine also published a contrasting opinion piece arguing there is little evidence of danger, and its editorial stance was to present a debate rather than issue an official warning. The claim originates from naturalnews.com, a site with a history of misrepresenting scientific content. Thus, while it is true that a Scientific American article contained a warning, the claim that “Scientific American magazine warned” implies an institutional endorsement, which is misleading because the warning came from an individual author in an opinion piece, not from the magazine itself.

The evidence is clear and sufficient to decide. The claim is technically true in that a warning appeared in the magazine, but it omits the crucial context that this was an opinion piece and not the magazine’s official position. Including that context would significantly change the impression. Therefore, the appropriate decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
Scientific American published an opinion piece by Joel M. Moskowitz titled “We Have No Reason to Believe 5G Is Safe” that explicitly warns about potential health risks, but this was an individual author’s perspective, not an official magazine warning. The magazine also ran a contrasting opinion piece arguing there is little evidence of danger, and its editorial stance was to present a debate rather than issue a definitive position [source](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/scientific-american-created-confusion-5gs-safety-clear-joel-moskowitz). The claim originated from naturalnews.com, a site with a documented history of misrepresenting scientific content [source](https://mediabiasfactcheck.com/natural-news/). Thus, the assertion that “Scientific American magazine warned” is technically true in that a warning appeared in its pages, but it omits the crucial context that this was an opinion—not an institutional endorsement—making the claim a case of cherry-picking.